WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:08.340
In 1966, Mao Zedong had a problem.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.780
The Chinese leader who had led a peasant army
to victory in the Chinese Civil War, and established

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:18.660
the communist People’s Republic of China
in 1949, was getting old.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.470
Worse, his radical policies had devastated
the country, and triggered the deadliest famine

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:25.090
known to human history.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:31.200
By the early 1960s, Mao’s once-great influence
and public presence were at an all time low,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.550
and there were rumors that he was dying, or
even dead already.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:39.030
He needed to find a way to seal his legacy
as the face of Chinese communism.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:43.260
And a new revolution to lead.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.760
It started in a river.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.579
The Great Leap Forward was a disaster.

00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:58.500
It was Mao’s 1958 plan to quickly industrialize
China by working its massive peasant population

00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:59.500
nonstop.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.850
ARCHIVE: He promises to transform the People’s
Republic into an instant paradise through

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.140
sheer force of numbers.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.590
Forcing workers in the countryside to farm
crops on government-run communes.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:15.729
And millions more to manufacture crude steel
in homemade blast furnaces.

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:18.550
And even though Mao told the world that the
plan was succeeding…

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:22.250
NARRATOR: Everywhere, the communists report,
production records are being broken.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.280
… the truth was much more desperate.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:29.180
ARCHIVE: They flood the fields, exhaust the
soil, and farm production instead of going

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.229
up, goes down.

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:34.799
The Chinese people were being forced to work
tirelessly on land they once owned themselves

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:37.560
— and they were starting to lose morale.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.909
And despite reports of widespread famine,
with millions of people starving to death,

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:44.040
Mao kept production quotas high.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.440
ARCHIVE: The pace grows more frantic.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.140
Ceaselessly, without rest, one observer writes.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.260
Mao's Great Leap Forward ended in 1962.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:00.650
By that time somewhere between 23 and 55 million
people had died in the famine.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.590
Over in the Soviet Union, a different political
upheaval was happening.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:09.220
Soviet leader Joseph Stalin, who Mao modeled
himself after, was dead.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:15.260
And Mao watched as Nikita Krushchev, Stalin’s
successor, implemented a period known as “de-Stalinization.”

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:19.850
Where Krushchev set out to reverse many of
Stalin’s policies and dismantle the personality

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:21.760
cult that had formed around him.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.850
Mao saw his own legacy potentially suffering
the same fate.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:29.390
His Communist Revolution was long over, and
his ideas weren’t taken as seriously after

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:31.230
the disaster of the Great Leap Forward.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:35.850
It was starting to look like Mao’s place
in the pantheon of powerful communist figures,

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:39.600
like Vladimir Lenin and Karl Marx, was in
peril.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:43.180
This is where the river comes in.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.220
Mao had a reputation for being a strong swimmer.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.220
And even used it as a symbol of his ideology.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:54.360
In 1956, he swam across China’s biggest
river, the Yangtze, in three highly-publicized

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.360
swims.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.460
To demonstrate that big things – like US
imperialism – didn’t intimidate him.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:05.510
10 years later, Mao took on the Yangtze again,
to dispel rumors of his failing health.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.630
This time with cheering crowds swimming alongside
him.

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:12.889
He brought his personal photographer, who
snapped this photo of the aging dictator in

00:03:12.889 --> 00:03:14.070
the river.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:18.799
And another one showing Mao waving to his
fellow swimmers, with the landmark Wuhan Yangtze

00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:20.699
River Bridge behind him.

00:03:20.699 --> 00:03:24.780
An iconic architectural achievement of the
communist government, and proof that he

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:25.780
was at the Yangtze.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:30.230
The swim made the front page of China’s
state newspaper, reporting that Mao swam around

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:35.200
15 kilometers, a little more than 9 miles,
in a span of 65 minutes.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.700
Which meant the 72-year-old would have shattered
world speed records.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.410
A lot of people outside of China laughed at
the outlandish story, but some saw the swim

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.780
for what it was: a sinister sign.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.160
Pointing out that Mao’s swims from a decade
earlier preceded the catastrophic Great Leap

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.170
Forward.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:55.790
Experts feared that Mao was on the verge of
kicking off another disastrous period of turmoil

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:57.800
in China.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.120
They were right.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.699
Two months before the swim, Mao had announced
the beginning of his Great Proletarian Cultural

00:04:04.699 --> 00:04:05.699
Revolution.

00:04:05.699 --> 00:04:10.120
A call to hunt down and eliminate the “bourgeoisie
who [had] sneaked into the party.”

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.479
Basically to purge the government of anyone
who strayed from principles of Maoism.

00:04:14.479 --> 00:04:17.290
And it kicked into high gear after his historic
swim.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:22.650
Which prompted a craze for swimming in swept
China, but more importantly, a craze for Mao.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:26.610
Especially among the group that Mao wanted
to influence the most: China’s youth.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:31.190
Writer Liang Heng recalled that seeing Mao
as “human flesh and blood” after the Yangtze

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:35.120
swim resolved him to “serve him with all
his heart.”

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.500
Millions of Chinese youth organized into the
fanatical Red Guards, a paramilitary force

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.100
concentrated mostly in Chinese cities.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:47.790
And, with Mao’s blessing, they wreaked havoc
in the name of Cultural Revolution.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.100
Their mission was to destroy the four olds:

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.280
ARCHIVE: Old culture.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:52.280
Old ideology.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:53.280
Old customs.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.280
Old traditions.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:05:01.500
The idea was basically to tear down the vestiges
of Imperial China and rewrite history centered

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.040
around Mao Zedong.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.390
Renaming buildings and streets, destroying
cultural sites, and violently humiliating,

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:13.620
and often torturing and murdering, anyone
they accused of opposing Mao’s ideas.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.320
Which they plastered all over the cities.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.790
And carried in their pockets in the form of
Mao’s “Little Red Book” a collection of his sayings and

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:22.930
principles.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:27.960
And although the violent Red Guards were basically
dissolved by 1969, the Cultural Revolution

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:32.320
is considered to have continued until Mao’s
death in 1976.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.650
Ending a decade of destruction that had elevated
the leader to god-like levels.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:42.090
And resulted in over 1 million people dead.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:46.000
The chaos of the Cultural Revolution scarred
China for generations.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.900
But Mao basically got what he wanted.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:53.120
Even though the Chinese Communist Party condemned
the Cultural Revolution in 1981, and Chinese

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.310
communism diverted away from Maoism, they
didn’t denounce Mao himself.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:02.120
The Cultural Revolution solidified Mao’s
cult of personality, and that influence has

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:03.420
lasted.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:08.520
Mao’s swim, which is still commemorated
each year in China, was more than a display

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:09.730
of strength.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:15.160
It was a message: to get behind Mao as he
began his last revolution.

